        "raw": "*The other day, I was rummaging around in my attic, and I found my old collection of Sentinel Comics Trading Cards from the ‘90s! So I thought I’d post them here for all of you.*\n\nOkay, so that didn’t actually happen. Sentinel Comics never made trading cards in the ‘90s. However, a [lesser-know comics company](https://www.marvel.com/) did actually make a series of trading cards. And on the backs of those cards were Power Ratings: numerical rankings of characters’ powers, skills, and abilities.\n\nSo, I thought that it would be fun to make Power Ratings for the Sentinel Comics characters.\n\nHere’s how the system works. There are six attributes: **Fighting Ability**, **Intelligence**, **Strength**, **Speed**, **Durability**, and **Energy Projection**. Every character’s attributes are ranked on a scale from 1 (below average) to 7 (godlike). In most cases, 2 is the human average. More details are available here: https://marvel.fandom.com/wiki/Power_Grid\n\nAnd here are my ratings for all of the Sentinel Comics heroes: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1iZ073schHrVxeoM3JEjjuQQe_mauKUt6ErpZfHA5ZqM/edit\n\nI may get around to the villains in the future; time will tell.\n\nOf course, we all know that trying to quantify and rank the superpowers of comic characters is a fool’s errand. The powers and abilities of such characters fluctuate wildly from issue to issue. Nonetheless, I did this just for fun, so here I am.\n\nWell, what do you think? Did I get the rankings right? Am I way off? Should Character X actually be stronger than Character Y? I’d love to hear your thoughts.\n\nLet the discussions and debates begin!",
